== Allergy Shots (Subcutaneous Immunotherapy - SCIT)

- Adults and kids can get it, though not typically recommended for children under age 5 - many pediatric patients dislike shots and may not cooperate.
- May take up to 1 year on maintenance dose to notice symptom improvement
- Phases: buildup, maintenance
  - Buildup: starting from tiny amounts of allergen, get slowly increasing amounts through weekly to twice weekly shots for around 4-6 months  
  - Maintenance: once the effective dose is reached - this may vary per patient and by allergen. Shots become usually monthly.
- shots must be given in a supervised environment and you have to wait 30 mins after the shot for monitoring in case of reaction
- risks: ~40% for large local reactions, 1/2000 per shot for anaphylaxis. Mortality rate for anaphylaxis is 1 in 2 million
- 7 confirmed fatalities across 54.4 million injection visits, from 2008-2016

== Sublingual tablets (Sublingual immunotherapy - SLIT)

- available only for: dust mite, birch, grass, and ragweed
- sublingual = tablet placed under the tongue, quickly dissolves; contain the allergens above
- Daily home administration for 3 years; symptomatic relief expected within 6 months with lasting benefit after completion of full course. Should be paused/avoided in setting of oral ulceration or uncontrolled asthma
- The main risk is oral itch, swelling, discomfort, which tends to improve after a few weeks/months of therapy; anaphylaxis exceedingly rare
  - Non-drowsy Antihistamines can be given one hour beforehand if needed
- First dose given in clinic 
